  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 240 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nAs the thermal ridge nudges further eastward into the weekend and \nearly next work week, a stronger push of low-level southwesterly \nflow will bring warmer and more humid air into the Central Plains. \nThis pattern shift will allow for temperatures to rise significantly \nwith values through the 90s, especially Sunday and Monday. Dewpoint \nvalues topping out through the 70s, paired with these temperatures, \nwill lead to heat index values near 100-105+ degrees over western \nIowa Saturday, and much of the area Sunday and Monday. The upper \nlevel northwesterly flow into Iowa though in relation to the closer \nproximity of the thermal ridge will also introduce additional \nchances for showers and thunderstorms, particularly into Sunday \nmorning and again late Sunday into Monday. Smoke also could limit \nhighs a bit Saturday as guidance suggests a return through the day. \nAll important things to watch over the coming days, but at least in \nterms in heat headlines, the potential is certainly there for some \nareas of Central Iowa. The warm and moist airmass Sunday through \nearly next week will bring about to a substantial increase in \ninstability and favorable shear profiles to reintroduce severe \nweather potential as various shortwaves round this ridge into \nthe Midwest. At this time, guidance suggests, as mentioned \nearlier in this paragraph, early Sunday morning and especially \nlate Sunday into Monday morning with the increasing LLJ into \nIowa to be possible targets for some potential severe weather, \nwith the SPC highlighting north/northeast Iowa in a 15% risk. \nWill keep a close eye on this over the coming days as details \nbecome more clear. The thermal ridge by midweek next week moves \nvery little, with Iowa still in the ring of fire as additional \nwaves are indicated in long term guidance to pass through, \nkeeping the pattern more on the active side.",
